/* ---- THE ROUND-RECORD WALKER -- H7 -----------------------------------------
   THE ASSET-EXTRACTION BINDING.  If this decode is wrong the port ships 81 wrong
   maps, and because they are PLAUSIBLE-LOOKING maps nobody notices until an
   enemy walks through a floor.
   $8529 is the SIZE PROBE: $9F5C is RRA/RRA/RRA/AND $1F (the carry-in reaches
   only bits 5-7 and the mask kills them, so it is (i >> 3) & $1F whatever the
   flags hold), and `AND 7 / INC A / LD B,A / LD C,(HL) / RL C / DJNZ` leaves the
   CARRY holding BIT (7 - (i & 7)) -- MSB FIRST within each byte.
       CARRY SET   SHORT  39 bytes  14 wide, row advance $12, then the MIRROR
       CARRY CLEAR LONG   77 bytes  28 wide, row advance 4,  no mirror
   AND THE BIT BUDGET CLOSES ON THE NOSE, which is WHY the sizes are 39 and 77:
       LONG   22 rows x 28 = 616 bits = 77.0 bytes exactly
       SHORT  22 rows x 14 = 308 bits = 38.5 -> 39 bytes, 4 bits spare
   51 short + 30 long = 4,299 bytes, $61A8..$7272, eight bytes of slack before
   $727B, and round 81's record at $7226.
   *** THE BIT CURSOR DOES NOT RESTART EACH ROW. ***  E (bits left in C) is
   PUSHed and POPped around the row advance at $84FE/$8504 precisely so that it
   SURVIVES: the stream is CONTINUOUS across all 22 rows.  A port that re-aligns
   per row is right on row 1 and wrong from row 2 onward -- which looks like a
   map, so gate CELL VALUES, never a row count. */
const BB_REC = {
  TABLE: 0x61A8, BITMAP: 0x8543, SHORT_N: 0x27, LONG_N: 0x4D,
  CURSOR: 0x8528,      /* ($8528) -- the record cursor, 0-based           */
  PTR: 0x84B7,         /* the running pointer: the OPERAND of LD HL,(nn)  */
  WIDTH_OP: 0x84E5,    /* the operand of $84E4 LD B,n -- $1C or $0E       */
  ADV_OP: 0x8500,      /* the operand of $84FF LD DE,nn -- 4 or $12       */
  OPEN: 0x8309, SOLID: 0x830A,      /* H3's two derived attributes        */
  FIRST: 0xF842,       /* row TWO, column TWO                             */
  ROWS: 0x16, MIRROR_W: 0x0E,
};
/* BOTH OPERANDS ARE STATE, and a build of one class NEVER writes the other's
   pair -- which is visible in the images themselves: image_tape.bin holds
   $84E5 = $1C / $8500 = 4 and image.bin (a round-80 dump) holds $0E / $12,
   because they were caught on opposite branches. */

/* ---- THE PLATFORM TILE AND THE WALLS -- H8 ---------------------------------
   H7 turned 81 records into 616 COLLISION cells.  This is what turns those
   cells into PIXELS, and it is where the TWO WRITE MODES live.
   $824A blits 16x12 per solid cell, and it does NOT use one mode:
       main 8 rows   $8288 LD (HL),A   *** STORE *** the left byte
                     $828C OR (HL)     ***  OR   *** the right byte
       tail 4 rows   $82A6 / $82AB     ***  OR   *** BOTH
   and the LAST map row SKIPS THE TAIL ($8296 DEC L / JR z), so a cell there
   writes SIXTEEN bytes, not twenty-four.
   TWO OF THE THREE `OR (HL)` SITES ARE UNOBSERVABLE against the machine's own
   build, because the scan runs left to right: cell c's OR'd right byte is
   immediately STORED over by cell c+1's left byte, and at a run's right edge
   the target is still zero, where OR and STORE agree.  Only a background the
   tile DISAGREES with separates them, which is what the gate drives.
   $856C IS TWENTY-FOUR BYTES, not eight and not sixteen: DE runs CONTINUOUSLY
   through both loops, 8 rows x 2 plus 4 rows x 2.  Its EVEN bytes come from the
   tile via $830B's LDI loop; three of its ODD bytes are patched at $824A's
   entry and REPATCHED by $82C5, which fires exactly ONCE after the first row --
   which is what makes the top of a platform look different from its body. */
const BB_TILE = {
  IDX_OP: 0x83EC,      /* the operand of $83EB LD A,n: a BUILD counter mod 5,
                          carried and NEVER recomputed from the round (H0)     */
  PTR: 0x860D,         /* ($860D) := $6180 + 8 * idx                           */
  SRC: 0x6180,         /* five 8-byte patterns -- TAPE DATA at $E680..$E6A7,
                          put here by the $96AE cold-start relocation (H0), so
                          no asset and no departure: BB_RELOC already ships it  */
  BUF: 0x856C, BUF_N: 24,
  ROW_TAB: 0xFD44,     /* the SHADOW row table: $FD04 + $A000, entrywise, all 22 */
  ROWS: 0x16, COLS: 0x1C,
  WALL_OP: 0x833B,     /* the operand of $833A LD A,n -- STATE *and* ARGUMENT:
                          LD A,$1E / XOR $1E toggles it between $1E and $00,
                          and the value IS the column, so consecutive builds
                          draw the two walls in the opposite ORDER            */
  WALL_BUF: 0x5B00, WALL_N: 0x20,
  EXPAND_SEL: 0x860C,  /* H3's bit-7-of-palette flag picks the expansion       */
};

/* $83D9's bit interleave, as a 16-entry table: each NIBBLE is stretched to a
   whole byte by doubling every bit ($8 -> $C0, $7 -> $3F).  MEASURED against the
   machine over both branches of $8390. */
const BB_TILE_X = [0x00, 0x03, 0x0C, 0x0F, 0x30, 0x33, 0x3C, 0x3F,
                   0xC0, 0xC3, 0xCC, 0xCF, 0xF0, 0xF3, 0xFC, 0xFF];

/* ---- THE POST-$8216 HUD FURNITURE -- H9 ------------------------------------
   H1 retracted "NO PIXELS AT ALL" and found four painters that all run AFTER the
   repaint.  Each has a reason it cannot be gated the obvious way:
     $7FC0  *** "ROUND 1 ONLY" WAS WRONG, and B1 found it by looking for the
            ADDRESS instead of for one opcode form.*** `CC C0 7F` (CALL z at
            $7F58) is not its sole reference: $9998 is `C3 C0 7F`, a TAIL JUMP
            from the mid-game join, so the whole HUD is repainted whenever a
            second player joins, on ANY round.  A census of `C0 7F` finds both
            plus one false positive inside the sprite bank at $C19C.
            Its RRD loop makes eight ASCII digits from the four BCD bytes of the
            high score, and THREE RRDs PER BYTE RESTORE THE RECORD -- so
            "converts" and "consumes" are the same observation unless the three
            score records are poked APART.
     $806C  EVERY round, min(lives,5)+1 cells, into DISPLAY, SHADOW and
            ATTRIBUTES.  TWO SELF-MODS, and one register doing two jobs.
     $957E  WRITES NO PIXELS unless a letter has been collected, and the masks
            are $00 after every measured build -- an unseeded gate here is green
            and checks nothing (TRAPS #44).
     $9904  the banner restore, SHADOW -> DISPLAY, then attributes from bit 5 of
            the colour shadow.  IT TAKES ITS ADDRESS IN HL ($484C / $488C). */
const BB_HUDF = {
  P1_FLAGS: 0xFD87, P2_FLAGS: 0xFD91,     /* +7; bit 7 = IN PLAY ($8097 RET p) */
  P1_LIVES: 0xFDDC, P2_LIVES: 0xFDE6,
  P1_ROW: 0x50E0, P2_ROW: 0x50FF,         /* display row 23, and P2 walks LEFT  */
  FULL_SRC: 0x85A1,                       /* 8 data + 8 mask -- the life icon   */
  LAST_SRC: 0x85B1,                       /* data LDI'd from the TILE at runtime */
  WALK_OP: 0x80F6,                        /* $8098 patches this INC L / DEC L   */
  SHADOW_OP: 0x80C1,                      /* $80B9 patches $80C0's operand so the
                                             shadow receives the byte just written */
  INK_BIAS: 0x28,                         /* $80C9 SUB $28: the OPCODE doubles as
                                             the ink, 4 for P1 and 5 for P2     */
  EXT_P1: 0xFDE3, EXT_P2: 0xFDED,         /* SIX collected-letter bits (H6)     */
  EXT_TAB: 0x95D5, EXT_STRIDE: 5, EXT_N: 6,
  EXT_COL_OP: 0x95AE,                     /* the self-modified column offset    */
  SUPP: 0x81BB,                           /* $81B9's RET z -- ARMED $C8, off $00 */
  BANNER_A: 0x484C, BANNER_B: 0x488C,
  HIGH: 0xFFE1, ASCII: 0x85E9, ASCII_N: 8,
};
/* the life icon, 8 data + 8 MASK, from $85A1 -- a little bubble */
const BB_HUDF_ICON = [0x00, 0x00, 0x3C, 0x7E, 0x7E, 0x7E, 0x3C, 0x00, 0xFF, 0xC3, 0x81, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x81, 0xC3];
/* ...and the mask half of the "spent life" record at $85B9, whose DATA half is
   LDI'd from the tile buffer at runtime by $806C itself */
const BB_HUDF_LAST_MASK = [0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00];
/* THE EXTEND ROW'S GLYPHS ARE THE SAME SIX ROM CHARACTER-SET CELLS H6 FOUND IN
   $C068's bubble table -- $3E28, $3EC0, $3EA0, $3E28, $3E70, $3E20 = e,x,t,e,n,d.
   Two unrelated tables naming the same six addresses, so the DECLARED DEPARTURE
   already in place for the bubble letters covers this row as well: the port
   substitutes drawn letters and ships no ROM.  Entries are
   [display address, glyph source, attribute]. */
/* THE SUBSTITUTE EXTEND LETTERS -- a DECLARED DEPARTURE, drawn for this port by
   tools/make_letters.py, which refuses to emit unless every glyph differs from
   the ROM's (36 of 48 bytes do).  The original reads the Spectrum character set
   through $95D5's table, and H6 found the SAME SIX ADDRESSES in $C068's bubble
   table -- so one substitute serves both, and no ROM byte is shipped. */
const BB_LETTERS = [[0x00, 0x00, 0x7C, 0x6C, 0x7C, 0x60, 0x7C, 0x00], [0x00, 0x00, 0x6C, 0x38, 0x10, 0x38, 0x6C, 0x00], [0x00, 0x30, 0x7C, 0x30, 0x30, 0x30, 0x1C, 0x00], [0x00, 0x00, 0x7C, 0x6C, 0x7C, 0x60, 0x7C, 0x00], [0x00, 0x00, 0x78, 0x6C, 0x6C, 0x6C, 0x6C, 0x00], [0x00, 0x0C, 0x3C, 0x6C, 0x6C, 0x6C, 0x3C, 0x00]];

/* *** ONE FONT.  BB_HURRY_FONT USED TO SIT HERE. ***  It was a ten-cell list
   indexed by POSITION IN "HURRY UP !", which meant the port carried a second
   transliteration of $802E beside the general one -- two walks, two fonts and
   two places for the same behaviour to drift.  BB_BANNER_FONT below is indexed
   by CHARACTER CODE, as $8044 is, and serves all three strings.
   THE CHANGE IS PIXEL-NEUTRAL AND GATED AS SUCH: build/hurryfont.json is kept
   as a FROZEN RECORD of what M7.20 drew, no longer regenerated, and the suite
   asserts the surviving font still reproduces it cell for cell.  If the banner
   ever moves, that row is what says so.
   (The '!' whose first draft was byte for byte the ROM's -- TRAPS #136 -- is
   one of the seven codes that record holds.) */
/* *** THE BANNER FONT, INDEXED BY CHARACTER CODE. ***  $802E's $8044 builds
   every glyph address as $3C00 + 8*code -- the SPECTRUM ROM CHARACTER SET --
   and this project ships no ROM, so these cells are DRAWN FOR THIS PORT by
   tools/make_banner.py.

   *** THE COUNTS USED TO LIVE IN THIS COMMENT AND THEY WENT STALE. ***  It said
   "these FIFTEEN cells" and "67 of 112 bytes" while the fixture held 32 rows and
   160 of 248 -- wrong for several increments, with every gate green, because the
   gates compare this file's LITERAL against build/bannerfont.json and nothing
   read the prose.  The numbers now live in BB_BANNER_FONT_CENSUS below, which the
   suite asserts against the fixture, so a stale count is a RED ROW (TRAPS #202).

   *** THE TOOL'S ASSERT IS A MINIMUM, NOT MERELY AN INEQUALITY. ***  The first
   drafts of T and F differed from the ROM's cells in ONE and TWO bytes of eight
   -- enough to pass "not identical" and not enough to be a different face.  An
   inked glyph must now differ in at least THREE; see BB_BANNER_FONT_CENSUS for
   how many of how many do, and which glyph is closest.  The SPACE is eight zero
   bytes in any font and is exempt in both directions.
   build/bannerfont.json carries the evidence.

   The seven codes "HURRY UP !" uses keep make_hurry.py's exact bytes, so M7.20's
   banner does not move a pixel. */
/* THE FONT'S OWN NUMBERS, ASSERTED RATHER THAN DESCRIBED (TRAPS #202).  Every
   value here is checked against build/bannerfont.json by test/bb.test.js, so it
   cannot drift the way the prose above did.  It is DERIVED, not authoritative:
   the fixture is the measurement and this is the claim about it. */
const BB_BANNER_FONT_CENSUS = {
  codes: 60,            /* rows in build/bannerfont.json                       */
  bytes_total: 472,       /* 8 x the inked glyphs                                */
  bytes_differing: 292,   /* ...from the ROM cells they replace                  */
  inked: 59,            /* glyphs carrying ink; the SPACE is exempt            */
  closest: '.',         /* the FIRST, in code order, of the glyphs at the least
                           aligned distance -- the MIN_DIFF of 3 -- which several
                           reach; the census names one, the tool the rule      */
  /* M7.53 (the review's F7): the least distance of any inked glyph from ANY
     eight-byte window of the ROM (0 would be a glyph that IS ROM bytes -- M7.20's P
     was), and from its OWN cell moved -2..+2 rows.  make_banner.py refuses a font
     where either rule fails; these are its minimum COUNTS, never a ROM byte. */
  rom_window_min: 1,
  displaced_min: 3,
};

