/* $B5DD -- type 4's TALL arc, 32 more signed bytes, and the sixteen that follow
   it.  The arc family is contiguous: the player's jump arc $B57D (24), its spill
   $B595 (8), the hop's two at $B59D and $B5BD (32 each), then this one.
   WHAT FOLLOWS IT IS NOT AN ARC.  $B5FD and $B605 are the two players' KEY
   TABLES -- (half-row select, bit mask) pairs, read by $ABF8 and chosen by
   BIT 0,(IY+$5A).  P1's are $DF/$02 and $DF/$01 (O and P), $FB/$01 (Q) and
   $7F/$01 (space); P2's are $EF/$10, $08, $02, $01 (6, 7, 9, 0).
   They matter HERE because type 4's arc cursor (IY+8) runs 0..$20 inclusive
   while the table is 32 bytes, so its LAST airborne frame reads ONE BYTE PAST
   the end: $B5DD + $20 = $B5FD, the first byte of P1's key table, taken as a
   signed Y delta of -33.  (For the bit-7 arc the same overrun lands on $B5DD
   itself, -4.)  So redefining the keys changes how a type-4 enemy finishes its
   jump.
   *** THESE ARE NOT THE TAPE'S BYTES. ***  The tape holds sixteen ZEROS at $B5FD
   (M7.44a/KR1).  BB_KEYTAB is a capture emulator's redefine (P1: O P Q SPACE)
   plus the SINCLAIR preset "6790" (P2) -- the table keyreader_fixture's S9 drives.
   The faithful cold start is the zeros, then front-end group 3's control select
   ($95F3 copies $F5D2 -> $B5FD via the $9601 LDIR).  Until group 3 lands,
   reset() seeds these as a STATED KNOWN GAP (not a flag): the reader
   (playerRead, $ABF4) walks them, so P1 plays on O/P/Q/SPACE and the arrow keys
   (5-8, the platform's cursor mapping) do not move P1 -- though in a 2P game
   ArrowDown/ArrowUp ARE 6/7, P2's LEFT and RIGHT here. */
